WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
I think back in 2012, you guys invested in Bitcoin.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:03.760
What was the thesis back then?

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:09.440
So the thesis in 2012 was digital gold. Gold is scarce, but it's not a fixed supply.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.123
Whereas Bitcoin is actually fixed at 21 million, and that supply will never increase

00:00:14.123 --> 00:00:19.600
no matter how much demand there is. I guess the one thing it doesn't was the privacy factor.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.160
Satoshi was talking about zero-knowledge proofs, but he didn't know how to implement them.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.480
And Zcash completes the arc because it's decentralized money that is private.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.240
How has your conviction in Zcash evolved with AI?

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.600
What is it looking like today?

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.800
I would say that it only...

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.320
Hey guys, it's good to be here.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.400
Yeah, hey Max. Good to be here as well.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.640
Yeah, so... And where are we? I mean, we're here in Singapore.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.045
And I guess my first question was, you guys invested, I think,

00:00:49.045 --> 00:00:52.880
by 2012 first in Bitcoin. What was the thesis back then?

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:58.480
So the thesis in 2012 was digital gold. When we looked at the properties of Bitcoin,

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:04.480
and compared them to gold, so the supply, it either equaled or exceeded gold.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.360
So supply, for example, gold is scarce, but it's not a fixed supply.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:14.455
Whereas Bitcoin is actually fixed at 21 million, and that supply will never increase

00:01:14.455 --> 00:01:17.040
no matter how much demand there is.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.440
It's more portable than gold. Gold bars are actually pretty heavy.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.200
That's what we call the die-hard problem.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.400
Like, you can sort of rob the Federal Reserve.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.760
You have to load it up with trucks. And then somehow cart it out of the city.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.760
It's actually pretty hard to move large quantities of gold.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.920
And the divisibility is greater.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.720
So if you just go down the characteristics, I think we charted out nine.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.991
Bitcoin exceeded gold. I guess the one thing it doesn't, and at the time we didn't probably

00:01:48.991 --> 00:01:52.480
give it a ton of attention, was the privacy factor.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.920
If you have a bar of gold, nobody really knows that except for you.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.920
And that is not the case with Bitcoin.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:03.120
And it was clear that Bitcoin was an open public blockchain back in the day.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:10.320
And it was always sort of a misconception that Bitcoin was used for nefarious activities

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:16.300
or bad actors. And one of our sort of pushbacks on that was that Bitcoin,

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.560
it's a public blockchain. It's auditable.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.240
It's actually a really bad place to do bad activity.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.600
Yeah. Actions are recorded forever, permanent.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:26.240
So, yeah.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.374
Yeah. To a fault. I think the other dimension is the technology.

00:02:31.374 --> 00:02:37.487
So Bitcoin works like your email, and it was the first money that was purpose-built for

00:02:37.487 --> 00:02:40.779
the internet. And so Bitcoin is actually two things.

00:02:40.779 --> 00:02:47.420
It's a protocol, Bitcoin capital B, and it's also an asset, Bitcoin lowercase b,

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:48.753
of the protocol.

00:02:48.753 --> 00:02:54.087
And so it is the first money that is really internet native,

00:02:54.087 --> 00:02:57.626
and that's very different than wire or ACH.

00:02:57.626 --> 00:03:04.166
And so the protocol and the technology aspect was a big aha moment for us.

00:03:04.166 --> 00:03:07.793
We said, hey, this is the internet of money.

00:03:07.793 --> 00:03:13.809
So this is a very big deal, and money is the greatest social network of all time.

00:03:13.809 --> 00:03:17.029
Right, right. That makes sense. Moving then forward to 2016,

00:03:17.029 --> 00:03:20.039
you participated in the seed round for Zcash.

00:03:20.039 --> 00:03:21.920
What was the thesis then?

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.530
It's the same thesis as it is today, that financial privacy is important.

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:30.820
And Bitcoin actually affords less than the traditional system,

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:35.347
ironically. If I wire you money, other than our banks and you and I,

00:03:35.347 --> 00:03:37.714
nobody else sees that movement of funds.

00:03:37.714 --> 00:03:44.565
And that is more private. Or if I use physical cash, that is more private than the Bitcoin blockchain.

00:03:44.565 --> 00:03:48.690
And so we saw that Zcash was solving that critical problem.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:51.040
And obviously, privacy, why is that important?

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:53.600
It's important for a free and open society.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.683
And I think Satoshi would have included it in the original protocol,

00:03:58.683 --> 00:04:02.876
if zero-knowledge proofs were further along, but they were brand new,

00:04:02.876 --> 00:04:07.203
they were much larger. I think there are size and issues in the block size.

00:04:07.203 --> 00:04:12.640
And so similar to like how the original internet would have included probably a money

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:17.493
over IP, or, you know, internet money, if it existed at that time,

00:04:17.493 --> 00:04:22.735
at the dawn of the internet, I think Satoshi would have put privacy into Bitcoin,

00:04:22.735 --> 00:04:28.198
because I think at the top, you know, the abstract of the white paper says electronic

00:04:28.198 --> 00:04:31.406
cash, but it's actually not cash, because it's public.

00:04:31.406 --> 00:04:37.450
So I think that we saw that problem, we felt it was an important thing to solve.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:40.950
And that was the thesis. And it's true to this day, even more so.

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:47.000
And this is, of course, pre the major breakthroughs in AI over the past couple of years.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:53.209
Yeah, privacy is normal. And money has been private for most of its existence.

00:04:53.209 --> 00:05:00.232
If you go back to, let's say, I don't know, is it 10,000 years?

00:05:00.232 --> 00:05:05.250
Shells, precious metals, gold, paper backed by gold, or gold is a precious metal.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:11.226
But it's only in the last, let's say, 50 years or so when we started making money digital,

00:05:11.226 --> 00:05:15.684
that it was so traceable and so public in a way.

00:05:15.684 --> 00:05:18.932
Yes, I think Satoshi was talking about zero-knowledge proofs,

00:05:18.932 --> 00:05:21.642
but he didn't know how to implement them.

00:05:21.642 --> 00:05:27.008
And so that's where I think we're kind of completing that arc.

00:05:27.008 --> 00:05:30.866
True, like you said, electronic cash, true cypherpunk ethos.

00:05:30.866 --> 00:05:38.000
And money has been misused by governments to manipulate populations forever,

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.917
either through capital controls or abuse through hyperinflation.

00:05:42.917 --> 00:05:49.263
And so they kind of achieve the separation of money and state,

00:05:49.263 --> 00:05:54.100
where the state no longer has a money monopoly.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:06:01.080
Imagine if the state had a monopoly on math and innovation, and that wouldn't go that well.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:06.366
The separation of money and state is important, but the true separation of money and

00:06:06.366 --> 00:06:12.285
state is making that money private. Because it's one thing if money is separated and

00:06:12.285 --> 00:06:17.781
it's Bitcoin, but if the government can surveil it and it's a panopticon,

00:06:17.781 --> 00:06:21.180
then you haven't fully achieved that separation.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:26.660
And so I think of privacy as this boundary where the state ends and you begin.

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:31.840
Your individual freedom, your self-sovereignty, those boundaries are important.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:36.455
And we see this with property. We see this in the Fourth Amendment,

00:06:36.455 --> 00:06:40.700
the unreasonable search and seizures of the government of your property.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:46.231
I think to some extent, obviously, gun rights backstop the First Amendment,

00:06:46.231 --> 00:06:51.180
but they also help you preserve your sovereignty against crime.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:55.080
Tyranny. It's the difference between citizen and subject.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:58.640
And the private money also achieves that.

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:03.960
And encryption is the peaceful, nonviolent version of the Second Amendment.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:12.460
So when you think of the values of the founding fathers and the country and what makes

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:16.460
America great, privacy, self-sovereignty, property rights, your boundary,

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:21.180
your space, including around your money, is super important.

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:27.544
And we always have to fight to preserve that and curb the encroachment of government,

00:07:27.544 --> 00:07:33.660
which only goes in one direction, but it never goes the other way.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:40.580
And I think Milton Friedman said that there is nothing so permanent as a temporary government program.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:41.400
Yeah, that makes sense.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:44.480
So we have to always back and stay vigilant.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:50.152
So cash was centralized but private. Bitcoin took a big leap forward in creating decentralized

00:07:50.152 --> 00:07:56.976
money, but it's public and Zcash completes the arc because it's decentralized money that

00:07:56.976 --> 00:07:57.480
is private.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:04.216
Right, very well put. How has your conviction in Zcash evolved with AI,

00:08:04.216 --> 00:08:06.600
with quantum? What is it looking like today?

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:14.314
I would say that it's an accelerant. And so AI lays bare how public the Bitcoin blockchain

00:08:14.314 --> 00:08:18.300
is or many blockchains that aren't private.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:26.828
The advances in quantum will only make Bitcoin less secure because it is not Shor resistant

00:08:26.828 --> 00:08:34.679
and Shor is an algorithm that will speed up the breaking of RSA or elliptical curve,

00:08:34.679 --> 00:08:35.660
ECDSA, cryptography.

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:41.768
And so Bitcoin is underpinned by the kind of cryptography that advancements in quantum,

00:08:41.768 --> 00:08:47.440
they're not Shor resistant and quantum, Shor is a quantum algorithm to break.

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:50.740
I think it's either you're factoring primes, large integers.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:54.160
You're factoring primes and then discrete logarithm problem.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:09:00.820
Whereas Zcash is quantum resistant to Shor's because you can't break what you can't see.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:05.697
And of course, it's prident. So if you use the shielded pool like Ironwood,

00:09:05.697 --> 00:09:11.380
no matter how good the AI is, it's not going to be able to surveil that.

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:14.846
I think if you're bullish on AI, you have to be bullish on Zcash,

00:09:14.846 --> 00:09:19.302
even if you don't know it, because if you believe that AI is going to continue to accelerate

00:09:19.302 --> 00:09:24.500
or improve, then that means your financial privacy will continue to deteriorate and therefore

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:26.700
Zcash will solve that problem.

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:32.240
That makes sense. What's the place of Bitcoin in a world where Zcash exists?

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:38.417
I mean, one could argue that had Bitcoin come after Zcash, no one would be using Bitcoin,

00:09:38.417 --> 00:09:39.540
right? What does that look like?

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:45.540
So I think there's some use cases where being publicly verifiable or auditable is actually

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:50.340
a feature, not a bug. And so there's going to be some institutions that want to say,

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:53.840
hey, here's our Bitcoin address. Take a look at our balances.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:58.840
This is what we have. Don't take it from us, but look at the blockchain.

00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:04.208
So I think there's scenarios, maybe central banks or some institutions want to hold their

00:10:04.208 --> 00:10:05.640
balances in that way.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:07.840
And also, I think Bitcoin is probably not going anywhere.

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:13.340
It's sort of started this revolution. It's got almost two decades of track record.

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:20.140
And for a lot of people, just having exposure to a store of value that has a fixed supply

00:10:20.140 --> 00:10:27.075
is enough. And that's great. But I think if you're going to actually use something for

00:10:27.075 --> 00:10:31.914
transactions and cash, then you're probably swapping out of Bitcoin into Zcash,

00:10:31.914 --> 00:10:35.140
transacting, and maybe you're going to swap back.

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:38.783
And I still think that the Bitcoin story, even though for us,

00:10:38.783 --> 00:10:42.426
we've been living it for, you know, close to probably, you know,

00:10:42.426 --> 00:10:46.340
15 years or so. It's still early for a lot of people.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:49.940
And so I think Bitcoin story is early and Zcash is very early.

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:53.440
Where do you see Zcash in say like five, 10 years from now?

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:57.940
Does it like follow the Bitcoin trajectory of like cycle after cycle getting adoption?

00:10:57.940 --> 00:10:59.440
Like, how do you think of that?

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:05.703
Yeah, I mean, I think the first major moment I think we experienced in Bitcoin was the

00:11:05.703 --> 00:11:09.849
Cyprus bail-in. And while people weren't necessarily in Bitcoin at that time,

00:11:09.849 --> 00:11:13.940
a lot of people, they saw what happened were basically deposit holders.

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:19.740
I think above 100,000 euros in Cyprus and banks, they got a haircut.

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:21.240
And it was taken away.

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:28.398
And I think that was one of the big inflection points for the general awareness and consciousness

00:11:28.398 --> 00:11:33.740
of Bitcoin. Why this decentralized non-governmental money is really important.

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:40.481
And I think that there's probably going to be moments like that in Zcash where the importance

00:11:40.481 --> 00:11:45.240
of financial privacy will be sort of illustrated in very clear terms.

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:49.540
And so there will be moments like that, like whether it follows a cycle.

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:54.640
A four year cycle like Bitcoin, I think is unclear, but it's probably most correlated

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:58.040
to advancements in AI, in which case it's its own.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:00.840
It doesn't the emissions cycle of four year.

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:07.156
The halving of the block reward is maybe less important than breakthroughs in AI and

00:12:07.156 --> 00:12:13.507
advancements in those models. It may just be much more tethered to those advancements

00:12:13.507 --> 00:12:15.840
than the actual halving cycle.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:19.340
Yeah, I would never consider Zcash a trade.

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:23.340
But it is the other side of the AI coin.

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:32.650
And so the more parabolic and meteoric the rise of AI, the more scarce an asset privacy

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:40.778
is going to become. And so I think if you're looking for the AI hedge in crypto or the

00:12:40.778 --> 00:12:44.340
quantum hedge in crypto, that's going to be Zcash.

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:53.140
And as AI continues to grow at the pace of Zcash and capture the minds of the world,

00:12:53.140 --> 00:13:01.416
I think Zcash is on that same wave. So I don't know that we're going to see the same

00:13:01.416 --> 00:13:08.526
cycles that we saw in Bitcoin. There was no, the transformer hadn't been invented in

00:13:08.526 --> 00:13:13.140
2012 or 2013 or 14. And so these catalysts weren't there.

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:17.740
There wasn't the quantum catalyst. There wasn't the AI catalyst.

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:25.644
And so I think it's hard to predict. I think it would be probably a mistake to say,

00:13:25.644 --> 00:13:31.240
okay, well, we'll just look at Bitcoin's chart and it will mirror Zcash.

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:33.840
I think we're in unchartered territory.

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:39.090
There's a trillion dollars committed in data center build outs by the frontier labs over

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:42.940
the next, I don't know what the period of time is.

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:44.940
It's probably the next five years, let's say.

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:48.240
Five years. It's maybe the biggest swing.

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:52.390
The country. More than the commercial internet, laying fiber optic,

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:56.040
more than railroads, more than the telecom revolution.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:14:00.040
I mean, this is the biggest technology infrastructure build out ever.

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:04.759
Yeah. So if we're right about that being a catalyst for Zcash,

00:14:04.759 --> 00:14:10.880
it's quite a big catalyst. But I think one of the easy ways to sort of think about Zcash

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:16.500
is also in reference to Bitcoin. And right now, I think the market cap of Zcash is somewhere

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:18.540
around $25 billion. And I think it's about $2 billion.

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:22.140
It's about 2% of the size of the Bitcoin market cap.

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:26.940
So what is the value of privacy? Is it 10% of Bitcoin?

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:30.640
And we love Bitcoin and I don't think it's going anywhere.

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:34.740
But I think that privacy is probably worth more than 2% of Bitcoin.

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:38.759
And so let's say it's worth 10%. Then you have a 5X, you know,

00:14:38.759 --> 00:14:40.840
you can sort of back into that very easily.

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:46.540
And you find yourself, I think, easily above $5,000 a coin.

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:49.940
And there's clearly a path to it. I think a $10,000 coin.

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:55.505
And just to build on that point, I'm not sure a lot of people know this,

00:14:55.505 --> 00:15:00.640
but Zcash is a fork of Bitcoin. And so it's a private Bitcoin.

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:04.940
It's encrypted Bitcoin. It's not starting from scratch.

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:07.640
It's standing on the shoulder of giants.

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:14.031
And so to think of it as a percentage of Bitcoin is, I think,

00:15:14.031 --> 00:15:19.540
a good and reasonable framework because its origin is, ultimately,

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:20.940
Bitcoin.

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:24.740
Obviously, it's much different in terms of where it's gone.

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:29.740
But I think matching it as a percentage of Bitcoin is a good way to think about it.

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:32.140
Because they both have 21 million supply.

00:15:32.140 --> 00:15:35.440
They both have the four-year halving cycle.

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:39.040
And so people who understand Bitcoin, you understand Zcash.

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:41.740
It's essentially encrypted Bitcoins.

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:44.240
Right. But they sort of left that part out.

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:51.440
But the forking of one coiner. Obviously, Zcash has zero-knowledge proofs and it's very

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:57.140
different technology. But the important things like fixed supply of 21 million coins.

00:15:57.140 --> 00:15:58.940
There will never be more.

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:03.840
And going back to the early part of the conversation of comparing Bitcoin to gold.

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:09.240
Gold is scarce. We're still finding and mining more gold.

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:12.640
You can't mine more than 21 million Bitcoin.

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:17.740
You will never be able to mine more than 21 million Zcash.

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:22.054
A lot of people don't understand that. And when you tell them that simple fact,

00:16:22.054 --> 00:16:23.640
they're like, aha, I get it.

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:26.640
Because gold is actually abundance in the universe, right?

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:29.440
Right. Yeah, we've made this point.

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:33.140
Exactly. We made this point years ago. And people are like, what are you talking about?

00:16:33.140 --> 00:16:37.340
It's actually very serious. Yeah, there's like trillions and trillions of dollars of

00:16:37.340 --> 00:16:40.240
gold floating on asteroids, you know, right around us.

00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:45.040
And it's actually as plentiful as sand in the universe.

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:49.640
It's only scarce on Earth. They're precious on Earth.

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:52.240
So we've made this point, but I think it bears repeating.

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:52.740
Yeah.

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:57.240
Yeah, it was a little too early in 2020 when we first made this point.

00:16:57.240 --> 00:17:01.840
And people's heads exploded. And they said, that's crazy.

00:17:01.840 --> 00:17:04.240
That's too sci-fi. What are you talking about?

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:10.593
But today, people are more open to that idea because Elon's talking about data centers

00:17:10.593 --> 00:17:16.057
in space. Well, if we can do that, why can't we get to an asteroid and put a probe on

00:17:16.057 --> 00:17:17.340
it and potentially mine it?

00:17:17.340 --> 00:17:23.107
And so I think the time is right. And I've started to hear other people make this point

00:17:23.107 --> 00:17:25.740
on X. And people are like, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:27.440
But you heard it first.

00:17:27.440 --> 00:17:29.840
Yeah. The OGs, yeah.

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:33.140
A little over a year ago, you started Cypherpunk.

00:17:33.140 --> 00:17:35.440
That was, in retrospect, exceptional timing.

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:39.840
What was the decision factor in starting it at that time specifically?

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:44.240
So we wanted to give people access to Zcash.

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:48.840
So Gemini listed Zcash in 2020. And then in 2018.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:53.040
And so I think we were probably the first regulated exchange ever to list it.

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:57.640
And we were very proud of that. And we've been supporting Zcash ever since.

00:17:57.640 --> 00:18:02.540
But a lot of people, they need to access assets through an equity wrapper,

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:10.740
let's say. So we wanted to increase the ability for people to gain exposure to Zcash.

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:16.940
So we started by essentially buying Zcash and then expanding.

00:18:16.940 --> 00:18:19.640
And that's how we became Cypherpunk, the company.

00:18:19.640 --> 00:18:26.640
The goal is to support cypherpunk ethos and invest in privacy technologies.

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:31.384
Right. So it's definitely not a DAT. It started by buying Zcash,

00:18:31.384 --> 00:18:38.700
but now we're mining. And ultimately, we want to support, invest in,

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:44.730
foster privacy-promoting technology, technologies that promote the First Amendment,

00:18:44.730 --> 00:18:48.840
the Second Amendment, the Fourth Amendment, American values.

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:54.840
So it starts with Zcash, then it's evolved to Zcash, Zcash mining.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:19:00.340
But really anything privacy, any privacy technology is on the table.

00:19:00.340 --> 00:19:04.640
So how do you think of the politics around Zcash?

00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:09.240
I mean, obviously, there's been like a war on crypto in the past.

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:12.340
Is there a risk of having like a war on privacy?

00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:15.240
I mean, I'm thinking, for example, of Flock cameras.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:15.740
Yeah.

00:19:15.740 --> 00:19:16.540
What are your thoughts on that?

00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:19.740
I think there probably will be a war on privacy.

00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:26.640
The question is not if, it's when. But I think that Zcash is unstoppable private money.

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:29.140
And we made the same arguments about Bitcoin.

00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:33.809
It's very hard to stop Bitcoin. It's easier than Zcash to stop Bitcoin,

00:19:33.809 --> 00:19:37.240
but it is very hard to stop decentralized money.

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:39.340
You pretty much have to stop the internet.

00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:44.040
So I think we will win the war, but I think there unfortunately probably will be one.

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:46.340
I agree. We were actually talking about this earlier.

00:19:46.340 --> 00:19:52.940
Today, the war on crypto happened. It was, I don't know, maybe it was four years.

00:19:52.940 --> 00:19:57.240
But there already is a war, a quiet war, let's say, on privacy.

00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:03.440
So going to the Flock cameras, a lot of people don't actually understand what they're doing.

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:09.917
They think they've confused them with the cameras that take a picture of your license

00:20:09.917 --> 00:20:12.940
plate after you've broken the speed limit.

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:16.140
And they've conflated the two. They think they're the same.

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:22.772
It's completely different because one is focusing on your license plate and after you've

00:20:22.772 --> 00:20:31.740
allegedly broken the speed limit. The other is surveilling everything and there's no trigger.

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:39.740
There's no allegedly breaking the law. It's just always on and always surveilling.

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:45.940
I've had a number of conversations where people thought they were the same thing.

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:50.940
It started with the license plate and speeding.

00:20:50.940 --> 00:20:56.273
You can argue that, fine, but let's just table that argument whether that should even

00:20:56.273 --> 00:21:02.620
happen or not. And of course, it only moved and progressed into we'll surveil everything,

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:06.140
not just the license plate, but the car.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:10.740
And law-abiding citizens who are just going to work and going about their daily lives.

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:15.740
And we're also going to put it in a centralized database.

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:22.921
It's all stitched together. It's not like, hey, here's the traffic light camera of one

00:21:22.921 --> 00:21:30.031
municipality or city or town. It's sort of strung all together in a centralized database

00:21:30.031 --> 00:21:35.740
with AI tooling, right, where you can just put it together even better.

00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:39.740
And ultimately, we know where that ends.

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:45.540
When you centralize data, it's a good target and people are going to want to hack it.

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:50.640
So you have all this information on people that's just sitting and being collected by

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:54.040
the government, and that's probably not going to end well.

00:21:54.040 --> 00:21:58.440
I guess how we got to that was the war on privacy is always happening.

00:21:58.440 --> 00:22:06.240
Governments only want to get more powerful, larger, trample on your rights.

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:15.340
That's just the only direction it goes. But will the war on privacy be explicit?

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:17.840
Like the war on crypto?

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:24.440
Yes. I mean, if history is any indication, I think that just like the internet,

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:28.840
the government fought encryption and they treated it as a munition.

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:36.007
And they're completely wrong. And then eventually they understood that encryption is

00:22:36.007 --> 00:22:43.340
important and the internet can only thrive and work if there actually is encryption on it.

00:22:43.340 --> 00:22:50.640
So we analogize Zcash to -- or let's say Bitcoin is HTTP.

00:22:50.640 --> 00:22:55.640
It's Web 1 where you put your credit card in to buy a plane ticket.

00:22:55.640 --> 00:23:02.140
It's in the clear. Everyone can read your information, your PII.

00:23:02.140 --> 00:23:09.640
And then Zcash is like HTTPS, secure, SSL, TLS, encrypted.

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:13.140
And the government fought this for a while.

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:20.203
And then eventually it completed. It completely changed its tune and 180ed on this and

00:23:20.203 --> 00:23:24.940
said, oh, all government websites must actually be HTTPS.

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:31.940
Right. So there's probably good reason to believe that they will start fighting it.

00:23:31.940 --> 00:23:37.940
They'll realize it's inevitable. They can't shut it down without shutting down the internet.

00:23:37.940 --> 00:23:43.967
Can't really do that. And then they'll accept the fact that they have to work and live

00:23:43.967 --> 00:23:47.740
with it. So ultimately I think we win, but I think there will be a fight.

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:54.740
Yeah, I think ultimately the government will realize that Zcash is safer for citizens.

00:23:54.740 --> 00:23:58.740
And they will come around to that. But it will probably take time.

00:23:58.740 --> 00:24:03.990
But if we look at the number of attacks in crypto, pipe wrench attacks,

00:24:03.990 --> 00:24:07.740
I think there's been 40 in France alone this year.

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:12.740
And so having your money open on the blockchain, is it dangerous?

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:14.540
It's not good for your citizens.

00:24:14.540 --> 00:24:21.540
The same is a centralized large surveillance database on your citizens' movements and whereabouts.

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:26.267
That's also not a good thing. And I think the government hopefully will come around to

00:24:26.267 --> 00:24:30.631
the idea that it's actually not a good thing to centralize all this information or be

00:24:30.631 --> 00:24:32.540
so public and so open about it.

00:24:32.540 --> 00:24:34.540
But it will take time, to Tyler's point.

00:24:34.540 --> 00:24:40.969
Like encryption, that arc took a while. And now governments will advise citizens to use

00:24:40.969 --> 00:24:43.540
things like encryption to protect themselves.

00:24:43.540 --> 00:24:49.340
Yeah. They still probably hate it, but have accepted that it's the better alternative.

00:24:49.340 --> 00:24:52.340
And I guess there's also like little they can do against this.

00:24:52.340 --> 00:24:59.992
I mean, I think I remember people printing PGP keys on paper because it's basically free

00:24:59.992 --> 00:25:03.340
speech and circumventing these restrictions that way.

00:25:03.340 --> 00:25:07.090
And similarly, it's very hard to stop Zcash and technologies like Bitcoin,

00:25:07.090 --> 00:25:08.340
as you said, Cameron.

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:13.818
I guess as a final question, one of the general remarks people have with regards to privacy

00:25:13.818 --> 00:25:18.140
is, "Oh, I have nothing to hide." What's your rebuttal to that?

00:25:18.140 --> 00:25:25.140
I think of, so why did Satoshi not put his name to the white paper in Bitcoin?

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:31.140
And I think he's a great example where ideas like Bitcoin are dangerous.

00:25:31.140 --> 00:25:39.390
And he clearly realized that putting his own identity to Bitcoin was dangerous for him

00:25:39.390 --> 00:25:43.940
personally, from a safety perspective. And so...

00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:51.786
Or you can go back hundreds of years to Copernicus and his dangerous idea that the earth

00:25:51.786 --> 00:25:55.940
actually revolves around the sun and not vice versa.

00:25:55.940 --> 00:26:01.940
And that was a very dangerous idea when he first came up with it.

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:08.578
So there's many reasons why privacy fosters intellectual curiosity,

00:26:08.578 --> 00:26:13.740
discoveries, innovation, ideas that are cutting edge.

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:19.928
That challenge the status quo, challenge the government, challenge our worldview,

00:26:19.928 --> 00:26:25.740
how we think about things. And there's many, many examples of that.

00:26:25.740 --> 00:26:29.740
You know, we could just continue to look back in history along the way.

00:26:29.740 --> 00:26:32.740
But I think Satoshi is the most obvious example.

00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:39.285
And also Satoshi, I think the Bitcoin that Satoshi mined, he couldn't even move it at

00:26:39.285 --> 00:26:43.540
this point because it's sitting there. And it's just too dangerous.

00:26:43.540 --> 00:26:48.540
Too dangerous to move. So I think that those are the reasons I would say.

00:26:48.540 --> 00:26:51.540
Yeah, I mean, you might say like, oh, I don't need privacy.

00:26:51.540 --> 00:26:59.435
But you want people like Satoshi to have privacy because they might be inventing Bitcoin

00:26:59.435 --> 00:27:07.254
that you benefit from. And so, yes, like Bitcoin is very threatening to governments,

00:27:07.254 --> 00:27:13.340
central banks, power structures. And you may not have it without privacy.

00:27:13.340 --> 00:27:18.340
So a lot of Einstein's ideas challenged the establishment.

00:27:18.340 --> 00:27:22.340
And he actually wasn't in a university at the time.

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:27.340
He was working as a patent officer in Bern, Switzerland.

00:27:27.340 --> 00:27:33.601
So he didn't have to worry about ruffling feathers of the professors above him that might

00:27:33.601 --> 00:27:38.340
be upset and blackball him for tenure. He wasn't in that political structure.

00:27:38.340 --> 00:27:43.140
And that gave him freedom of thought, right, to think outside of the box,

00:27:43.140 --> 00:27:51.140
let's say. But also freedom from political retribution on his career path.

00:27:51.140 --> 00:27:58.140
And so ultimately, he didn't need privacy to publish his papers.

00:27:58.140 --> 00:28:00.140
But there still might have been resistance.

00:28:00.140 --> 00:28:06.140
And maybe he would have needed it if he was a professor in a university system.

00:28:06.140 --> 00:28:11.866
And so there's many reasons why privacy is important, even if you don't think you need

00:28:11.866 --> 00:28:12.940
it for yourself.

00:28:12.940 --> 00:28:19.440
Just like I really like what Snowden says about he has a riff on freedom of speech and

00:28:19.440 --> 00:28:24.857
saying that it's something about, like, I don't care about it because I have nothing

00:28:24.857 --> 00:28:29.764
good to say. I'm getting it wrong. But freedom of speech is important,

00:28:29.764 --> 00:28:34.940
even if you don't want to exercise it or you don't.

00:28:34.940 --> 00:28:39.940
Maybe you're not going on X and you just, you know, not using it.

00:28:39.940 --> 00:28:42.740
You still want that free marketplace of ideas.

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:48.740
You still want it to occur and to happen because it's better for society.

00:28:48.740 --> 00:28:50.740
It's better for outcomes. It's better for innovation.

00:28:50.740 --> 00:28:57.026
It's better for progress. It's more likely to build an iPhone that you may purchase and

00:28:57.026 --> 00:28:57.740
may use.

00:28:57.740 --> 00:29:02.828
And so there's all these knock on effects that make privacy,

00:29:02.828 --> 00:29:09.765
freedom of speech, freedom, liberty super valuable to you, even if you don't think you

00:29:09.765 --> 00:29:12.540
need to exercise them directly yourself.

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:14.540
And I think it avoids people from, like, self censoring.

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:21.852
If you think of, like, the current state of academia, like how many professors and intellectuals

00:29:21.852 --> 00:29:27.540
have to censor their actual beliefs and what they're doing because they'll get canceled.

00:29:27.540 --> 00:29:33.540
Exactly. So it's very important for freedom of thought and that type of inquiry.

00:29:33.540 --> 00:29:37.540
And it should, at a minimum, be sort of default privacy.

00:29:37.540 --> 00:29:42.340
And you can opt out. But default public is not where we want to be.

00:29:42.340 --> 00:29:47.340
It also forces people to engage on the idea.

00:29:47.340 --> 00:29:51.340
Right. So let's say Satoshi wasn't worried about his safety.

00:29:51.340 --> 00:29:56.590
But if you there's only one thing to look at, which is the source code,

00:29:56.590 --> 00:30:02.340
you can't attack the person. You can't, like, confuse the two or conflate the two.

00:30:02.340 --> 00:30:08.340
You have to focus on the idea and the contents and separate that.

00:30:08.340 --> 00:30:13.504
And that's super powerful because it's not about judging the person behind the idea,

00:30:13.504 --> 00:30:20.828
it's just you have to focus. You have no choice but to speak to the idea and the facts

00:30:20.828 --> 00:30:27.140
and its merit. And so privacy focuses the conversation to where it should be.

00:30:27.140 --> 00:30:32.140
Right. That makes sense. Yeah. And that's why, you know, people use pseuds on X.

00:30:32.140 --> 00:30:39.196
Maybe not because they want to not be public, but they just want you to have to address,

00:30:39.196 --> 00:30:41.940
like, what they're saying in the tweets.

00:30:41.940 --> 00:30:43.940
And just stick it there.

00:30:43.940 --> 00:30:45.940
Right. Yeah.

00:30:45.940 --> 00:30:47.940
Thank you, guys. It's been a pleasure.

00:30:47.940 --> 00:30:52.440
Thank you. Thanks for having us. Great questions.
